  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The town describes Bonporteau as "a small, wild, unspoiled beach": after an avenue of eucalyptus, tamarisk and palm trees, you reach a wide cove of fine sand edged with rocks and crystal-clear water. It is a sensitive natural area, cleaned by hand, in the heart of a marine protected area. The beach is not supervised.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the official rule published by the Cavalaire-sur-Mer tourist office (cavalairesurmer.fr), dogs — even on a leash — are banned on all of the town's beaches from 6am to 10pm from 1 April to 31 October, and from 10am to 5pm from 1 November to 31 March. Outside those slots, the town mentions no other restriction; keep your dog on a leash to be safe. In practice: in summer, the usable window is limited to the night (10pm-6am); in winter it is wider (5pm to 10am the next day). The same rule applies on all the town's beaches, including the <a href="/en/fiche-grande-plage-cavalaire">town-centre beach</a> a little further east. Access to Bonporteau is via a very steep path, closed to vehicles and two-wheelers and with no wheelchair access; there is no food on site.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in summer, your dog can only come between 10pm and 6am; off-season, the window runs from 5pm to 10am the next day. The access path is very steep: best for a dog in good shape, and bring water for him.</div>

      <h2>On site</h2>
      <p>The Cavalaire tourist office listing (updated on 1 September 2026 on the regional tourism board and Côte d'Azur France websites) classes Bonporteau among the places open every day all year and mentions a car park, picnic area and toilets. It also carries the mention "animals not accepted", completed by a sentence saying that dogs on a leash are admitted on all beaches from 9pm to 6am. On the sand, your dog therefore has no place during the day: plan instead an early-morning or evening walk according to the signs, and do not count on a swimming area reserved for dogs.</p>
      <p>The cove is a fine-sand beach edged with rocks, with no lifeguard post: the town's lifeguard posts are on the other beaches of the bay (town centre, Parc, Dauphins, Pardigon). The water is described as particularly clear; in summer, bring water and something for shade, as access is by an open path.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>Bonporteau is the starting point of the Fenouillet path, which crosses the Fenouillet valley on Conservatoire du littoral land (Corniche des Maures). According to the town's leaflet, it is a waymarked loop of about 2.5 km starting from Bonporteau beach or the Maison de la Nature, with parking along the RD 559. The Cavalaire walking map asks you to keep your dog on a leash. Note: from 21 June to 30 September, access to the Corniche des Maures massif is regulated because of the fire risk (risk-level map on the Var prefecture website, available every evening from 7pm).</p>
    </div>

